 pair into part of a pair-based hand. But with only singles outside the pair, you must draw or wait for a tile to complete a chow or pung.\n\nLooking at unshown tiles:\n- **T5 is gone**, **B5 is gone**, **W7/W5 limited** \u2014 many middle tiles are depleted.\n- **T1, T6, W6** are all still partially available.\n\nBut critically, observe:\n- You already have **three pungs** (F2, J2, T9) \u2014 worth **All Pungs (6 points)** base.\n- Adding any **fourth meld** (pung/kong/chow) completes the basic hand.\n- With **F2 (South Pung)**, **Prevailing Wind is East**, **Your Seat Wind is North (F4)** \u2014 so F2 is not Prevalent or Seat Wind.\n- However, you have **F3 (North Wind) pair**, which *is* your Seat Wind! If you keep it and win, you get **Seat Wind (2 points)**.\n- Discarding **F3** would lose this potential pattern.\n\nBest improvement path:\n- Draw any tile that forms a chow with existing singles:\n    - T1-T2-T3? T2 exists (2 unseen), T3 exists (1 unseen) \u2192 possible\n    - T5-T6-T7? T5 gone, T7 gone \u2192 impossible\n    - W4-W5-W6? W5 exists (1), W4 exists (2), W6 = yes \u2192 possible if draw W4/W5\n- But your best chance is **waiting to draw a tile that pairs with T1/T6/W6**, then discarding F3 later \u2014 but currently, **keeping F3 is better** because it supports **Seat Wind**.\n\nThus, safest discard is **T1** or **T6**.\n\nCompare danger:\n- **T1**: Across player discarded T5, T6 \u2014 seems safe.\n- Next player discarded T3, T2 \u2014 possibly building lower bamboo/chow?\n- Previous player had T3-T4-T5 and T6-T7-T8 \u2014 they may have completed those sets.\n- T1 hasn't been claimed recently \u2014 low risk.\n\nSimilarly, **T6**: \n- You discarded T6 earlier \u2014 no one claimed it.\n- Previous player had T6-T7-T8 \u2014 they already formed it.\n- T6 appears safer than W6 (which connects to W5-W6-W7).\n\nSo between T1 and T6, both are safe. But **T1 is more terminal**, and terminals are often used in hands \u2014 however, given W1/B1/T1 all heavily discarded, likely many players have completed their terminal sets.\n\nStill, **T6** has slightly higher connectivity (middle tile), but since T5 and T7 are mostly gone, it\u2019s less useful.\n\nTherefore, **T1 is marginally safer**.\n\nBut wait \u2014 you already discarded T1 once before (in history), and no one claimed \u2014 same with T6.\n\nBut you have **only one copy of each**, and cannot form any chow yet.\n\nNow consider: **if you keep F3 (pair)**, and draw any tile that makes a pung (e.g., another T1 \u2192 T1 pair, then wait for third), but unlikely.\n\nBetter strategy: **minimize loss of scoring potential**.\n\nBy discarding **F3**, you break your **Seat Wind pair**, losing guaranteed **2 points** if you win.\n\nWhereas discarding **T1 or T6** keeps the possibility of completing a **2-point pattern (Seat Wind)**.\n\nAlso, **you currently have**:\n- All Pungs (6 points): \u2714\ufe0f (three pungs, need one more meld)\n- Two Dragon Pungs (J2 + ?) \u2014 only one dragon pung \u2192 no\n- Mixed Shifted Pungs? No\n- Outside Hand? Possibly \u2014 if you make a fourth meld with terminal/honor\n\nBut if you complete a fourth pung (say, T1-T1-T1), then:\n- Four pungs \u2192 All Pungs (6 pts)\n- Plus Seat Wind (F3 pair) \u2192 2 pts\n- Possibly Pung of Terminals/Honors (T9 is terminal) \u2192 1 pt \u00d73 (for F2, J2, T9) \u2192 3 pts\n- Total: 6+2+3 = 11 \u2192 qualifies (\u22658)\n\nEven with just chows:\n- Suppose you draw W5 \u2192 can form W4-W5-W6? Do you have W4? No. Can\u2019t form.\n- Draw T2 \u2192 T1-T2-T3? Need T3 \u2014 possible\n- But chances are low.\n\nSo best bet is to **keep the F3 pair** for Seat Wind value.\n\nThus, do **not play F3**.\n\nBetween **T1, T6, W6**, we eliminate F3.\n\nNow among T1, T6, W6:\n\n- **W6**: Only two left, one already seen in your hand, and across player played W6 \u2192 possibly dangerous? But W5/W4 exist \u2014 maybe someone waiting on W4-W5-W6? Possible.\n- **T6**: Connected to dead end (T5/T7 gone) \u2192 very hard to use\n- **T1**: Terminal, but common in chows like 1-2-3\n\nBut **T6 is least likely to complete a useful chow** now because T5 and T7 are nearly gone.\n\nHence, **T6 is the safest discard**.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding T6 minimizes risk as T5 and T7 are largely depleted, making it difficult for opponents to use T6 in a chow. Keeping F3 preserves the potential for the \"Seat Wind\" pattern (2 points), which contributes to reaching the required 8-point minimum. T1 has slightly higher utility in forming 1-2-3 chows, while W6 could connect to existing W4/W5 tiles still in circulation. Thus, T6 is the optimal discard.\n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
